[HH-1] PUMP PARAMETRIZATION: scale invariance forces ONE universal ODE; the response
       (commutator) is normalization-free; the worldline density is Mellin-bilinear
========================================================================================
[1a] conformal-time pump equation - k^2 x universal w-ODE = 0
     => ANY scale-invariant linear local pump (gain ghat(k_phys/H), dispersion f(k_phys/H))
        reduces to ONE k-independent ODE:  g'' - 2 ghat(w) g' + c^2(1 + f(w)) g = 0.
        (ghat > 0 = physical-time gain; w-derivative sign flip since w runs backward.)
        Physical gain rate / H = w*ghat(w): H-paced, the (C5) structure, AUTOMATIC.
[1b] friction elimination residual = 0
     => g = e^Ghat psi, Ghat' = ghat:  psi'' + Omega2 psi = 0,
        Omega2 = c^2(1+f) + ghat' - ghat^2  [w-form; physical-time form -ghat'-ghat^2].
        NOTE the w-sign: with g'' - 2 ghat g', the dressing is e^{+Ghat}, growing toward
        large w removed... amplitude bookkeeping lives ONLY in e^{Ghat(w1)-Ghat(w2)}:
     basis (GL2) invariance of Cauchy propagator: residual = 0
     gain factorization G_c^g = e^(Ghat(w1)-Ghat(w2)) G_c^psi: residual = 0
     => the RESPONSE the worldline reads is the Cauchy propagator: normalization-FREE
        (no vacuum choice, no Wronskian normalization enters -- the Bogoliubov lemma's
        'dynamics object' made explicit). Gain enters ONLY via e^{Ghat(w1)-Ghat(w2)} and
        via Omega2 inside psi. WLOG scan space = {Ghat-factor} x {Hermitian Omega2 family}.
[1c] worldline Mellin-bilinear formula:
     (i) Plancherel pairing at tau = 0.7 - 0.5i: closed = -3.95569223e-02-1.41716161e-01j,
         Mellin side = -3.95569223e-02-1.41716161e-01j,  rel.diff = 3.38e-31
     (ii) nu =  0.4: bilinear rho_c = 2.7925268032e-01  vs  2 pi nu/c^2 = 2.7925268032e-01  rel.diff = 0.00e+00
     (ii) nu =  1.0: bilinear rho_c = 6.9813170080e-01  vs  2 pi nu/c^2 = 6.9813170080e-01  rel.diff = 1.41e-31
     (ii) nu =  3.7: bilinear rho_c = 2.5830872930e+00  vs  2 pi nu/c^2 = 2.5830872930e+00  rel.diff = 3.05e-31
     => rho_c(nu) = -2ic[phia~(nu)psib~(-nu) - phib~(nu)psia~(-nu)], free = 2 pi nu/c^2
        EXACTLY (the [2d] Planck-odd-part recovered through the bilinear). Convention for
        the scan: NORMALIZE rho_c by (2 pi/c^2) so free rho_c(nu) = nu; then the (C1) tail
        and the (C3) window A_max = 5.716 compare in agentEE [3d] units directly.
[1d] g-classes registered for the forward scan (STEP 3):
     P  power-law tails:        ghat or f = kappa w^{-p}, p in (0,2]; p = 2/3 distinguished
        (the WKB index-transfer candidate for omega^{1/3}); exactly solvable anchors:
        p = 2 (Bessel, complex kappa allowed), p = 1 (Coulomb/Whittaker).
     B  gain bands:             ghat = eps * bump((w - w0)/dw)  (compact, smooth);
        sharp-edged variant for the endpoint/power-law class.
     L  log-periodic:           ghat or f = eps cos(Omega ln w) x envelope.
     D  modified dispersion:    f real: f = kappa w^{-p} (phase-only at WKB order),
        omega^2 = c^2k^2(1 + f(k/aH)) the X2-named form.
     X  windowed filter bank:   ghat = sum_j eps_j bump(ln w - ln w_j) = B/L composite.
     G  Gevrey-3/stable class:  profiles with Mellin g~(mu) ~ e^{-A|mu|^{1/3}} (the
        index-1/3 stable-subordinator kernel in ln w) -- the inverse-problem candidate.
     For each: compute Delta rho_c(nu), fit ln|tail| = lnK + q ln nu - ct nu^s (s free),
     test s = 1/3, the cos(sqrt3 ct nu^{1/3}) lock, additivity, and one-sidedness.
